WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:07.660
It's time now to look how we get our clients into a server and then travel from that server to another server.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:13.460
In Unreal, there are two ways to travel, that being the seamless and non-seamless methods.

3
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.019
What these mean is more meaningful when regarding them from the client's perspective.

4
00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:22.820
Seamless travel means there will be no blocking operations when traveling to a new server.

5
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.280
However, on the other hand, non-seamless travel will cause a blocking call.

6
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.280
In terms of a client, the client is forced to disconnect from the server

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.740
and once all of that is done, it will reconnect back to the same server which will now have a new map to load into.

8
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.859
There are three scenarios in which non-seamless travel must occur.

9
00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:44.659
Firstly, when loading a map for the first time.

10
00:00:44.659 --> 00:00:48.289
Secondly, when connecting to a new server for the first time.

11
00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:52.799
And thirdly, when we end a multiplayer game and start a new one.

12
00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:57.000
There are three main functions that drive traveling in Unreal, so let's take a look at these.

13
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.799
The first function is the browse function within the UEngine.

14
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.599
It's essentially a hard reset when loading a new map and will always result in a non-seamless travel.

15
00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:11.200
It's going to cause the server to disconnect the current clients before traveling to the new map.

16
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.859
And it's worth noting that a dedicated server cannot travel to another server.

17
00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:22.280
In other words, we can't browse to another URL, we must browse to a local map.

18
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.879
Then we have the server travel which is held in the UWorld

19
00:01:24.879 --> 00:01:28.950
and the client travel which is held inside the player controller.

20
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.349
The server travel is of course server only and will cause the server to jump to a new level.

21
00:01:33.349 --> 00:01:35.980
Bringing all connected clients with it.

22
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.780
The server is in charge of calling this function so we can't call it from a client.

23
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.920
And it will call the client travel function on all its connected clients for us.

24
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.719
Following that we have client travel and if called from a client we will be traveling to a new server.

25
00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:55.120
If called from the server it will instruct the client to travel to the new map with them.

26
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.319
And importantly it will maintain the connection to the server.

27
00:01:58.319 --> 00:02:03.900
The client won't get disconnected and then reconnected, they will maintain their connection.

28
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:09.099
Actors can be recognized as persistent and will travel from the previous level to the new.

29
00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:14.099
This is extremely useful for certain actors that you may want to persist through your server move.

30
00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:17.099
By default these actors persist through the level, we have the game mode.

31
00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:22.979
As well as any actors picked up from the Get Seamless Travel actor list inside the game mode.

32
00:02:22.979 --> 00:02:28.469
As well as that we have all controllers that have valid player states on the server only.

33
00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:32.610
Also on the server we will get any player controllers.

34
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.409
In addition we will also get all local player controllers on both the server and the client.

35
00:02:36.409 --> 00:02:41.009
As well as any actors picked up from there Get Seamless Travel actor list too.
